      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Patients with bronchial asthma having been challenged with allergen develop various types of asthmatic response, such as immediate (IAR), late (LAR) or delayed (DYAR) response, due to different immunologic mechanisms. The DYAR, beginning 26-32 h, reaching maximum between 32 and 48 h and resolving within 56 h after the challenge, differs from IAR and LAR in clinical and immunologic features."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To investigate the changes in the serum concentrations of chemokines associated with the isolated form of DYAR."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "In 22 patients the repeated DYAR (p < 0.001) was supplemented with recording of blood cell counts and serum concentrations of chemokines before, and up to 72 h after the bronchial challenge by means of enzyme-linked immunoassay, (ELISA)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The DYAR was associated with (a) significantly increased serum concentrations (p < 0.05) of CCL 2, CCL 3, CCL 4, CCL 7, CCL 20, CXCL 1, CXCL 8, CXCL 9, CXCL 10 and CXCL 11, and (b) significantly decreased serum concentrations, (p < 0.05) of CCL 5, CCL 11, CCL 17, CCL 22, CCL 24 and CCL 26, as compared with their pre-challenge as well as the PBS control values. No significant chemokine changes were recorded during the PBS controls (p > 0.1)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "These results, together with changes in the blood cell counts, provide evidence for an involvement of activated Th(1), cells and NK cells (CCL-2, -3, -4, -20, CXCL-9,-10,-11), neutrophils (CCL-20, CXCL-1,-8) and monocytes (CCL-2,-3,-4, -7, CXCL-10), upon co-operation of other cell types, such as epithelial, endothelial and dendritic cells, in the immunologic mechanism(s) underlying the DYAR."
          }
        ]
      },
